<exercise>
	<title>Distance from a point to a plane defined by points</title>

	<statement><p>
		Determine the distance from point <m> S(3,5,-4) </m> to the plane that passes through points
		<m> P(-5,-6,-1), Q(-1,-1,4), R(-4,3,0) </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		Create a vector from the plane to <m> S </m> by using any one of <m> P,Q,R </m> as the initial point;
		we'll use <m> P </m>:
		<md> \uvec{u} = \abray{PS} = (8, 11, -3) </md>.
		Create two vectors parallel to the plane from the provided points:
		<md>
			<mrow> \uvec{v} \amp = \abray{PQ} = (4, 5, 5) \text{,} </mrow>
			<mrow> \uvec{w} \amp = \abray{PR} = (1, 9, 1) \text{,} </mrow>
		</md>
		Now use the cross product to create a normal vector to the plane:
		<md> \uvec{n} = \ucrossprod{v}{w} = (-40, 1, 31) </md>.
		The distance from <m> S </m> to the plane will be the norm of the orthogonal projection of <m> \uvec{u} </m> onto <m> \uvec{n} </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		So first compute that projection:
		<md> \uvec{p} = \uproj{u}{n} = \left( \frac{2680}{427}, - \frac{67}{427}, - \frac{2077}{427} \right) </md>.
		Finally, compute the distance from <m> S </m> to the plane as the norm of <m> \uvec{p} </m>:
		<md> d = \unorm{p} = 67 \, \sqrt{\frac{6}{427}} </md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

