<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis">
	<title>Orthogonal projection onto an axis in <m> \R^2 </m></title>

	<introduction>

		<p>
		Recall that the <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-std-basis" text="title" /></term> in <m> \R^2 </m> are
		<m> \uvec{e}_1 = (1,0) </m> and <m> \uvec{e}_2 = (0,1) </m>.
		</p>

		<p>
		In the following,
		consider general <m> \R^2 </m> vector <m> \uvec{u} = (x,y) </m>.
		</p>

	</introduction>

	<task xml:id="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e1">

		<statement><p>
			Express <m> \uvec{u} </m> as a decomposition into a sum of its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-parallel-component" text="custom">vector component parallel to <m> \uvec{e}_1 </m></xref></term>
			and its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthog-component" text="custom">vector component orthogonal to <m> \uvec{e}_1 </m></xref></term>.
			Then sketch a representative diagram in the first quadrant of the <m> xy </m>-plane that illustrates this decomposition using a vector addition triangle.
		</p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p> Parallel component: <m> \uvec{p} = \proj_{\uvec{e}_1} (x,y) = (x,0) </m>. </p>

			<p> Orthogonal component: <m> \uvec{n} = (x,y) - \proj_{\uvec{e}_1} (x,y) = (0,y) </m>. </p>

			<p> Decomposition: <m> (x,y) = (x,0) + (0,y) </m>. </p>

			<image width="32%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-decomp-R2-e1">
					<xi:include href="exercises.d/orthog-decomp-R2-e1.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition of a vector in two-dimensional space into a sum of its components parallel to and orthogonal to the first standard basis vector. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a right triangle of vectors in the first quadrant of the <m> xy </m>-plane.
					The legs of the right triangle are vector <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					drawn from the origin horizontally rightward along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (x,0) </m>,
					and vector <m> \uvec{n} </m>,
					drawn from the terminal point of <m> \uvec{p} </m> vertically upward to the point <m> P(x,y) </m>.
					The hypotenuse of the triangle is vector <m> \uvec{u} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> P(x,y) </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>

		</answer>

	</task>

	<task xml:id="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e2">

		<statement><p>
			Express <m> \uvec{u} </m> as a decomposition into a sum of its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-parallel-component" text="custom">vector component parallel to <m> \uvec{e}_2 </m></xref></term>
			and its
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthog-component" text="custom">vector component orthogonal to <m> \uvec{e}_2 </m></xref></term>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p> Parallel component: <m> \uvec{p} = \proj_{\uvec{e}_2} (x,y) = (0,y) </m>. </p>

			<p> Orthogonal component: <m> \uvec{n} = (x,y) - \proj_{\uvec{e}_2} (x,y) = (x,0) </m>. </p>

			<p> Decomposition: <m> (x,y) = (0,y) + (x,0) </m>. </p>

			<image width="34.4%">
				<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-orthog-decomp-R2-e2">
					<xi:include href="exercises.d/orthog-decomp-R2-e2.tex" parse="text" />
				</latex-image>
				<shortdescription> Diagram illustrating a decomposition of a vector in two-dimensional space into a sum of its components parallel to and orthogonal to the first standard basis vector. </shortdescription>
				<description><p>
					A diagram consisting of a right triangle of vectors in the first quadrant of the <m> xy </m>-plane.
					The legs of the right triangle are vector <m> \uvec{p} </m>,
					drawn from the origin horizontally rightward along the <m> x </m>-axis to the point <m> (x,0) </m>,
					and vector <m> \uvec{n} </m>,
					drawn from the terminal point of <m> \uvec{p} </m> vertically upward to the point <m> P(x,y) </m>.
					The hypotenuse of the triangle is vector <m> \uvec{u} </m>,
					drawn from the origin to the point <m> P(x,y) </m>.
				</p></description>
			</image>

		</answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			What is the connection between the results
			of <xref ref="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e1" text="type-local">part</xref>
			and <xref ref="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e2" text="type-local">part</xref>,
			and the discussion of <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-std-basis" text="title" /></term> presented in
			<xref ref="subsection-vectors-concepts-standard-basis" />?
		</p></statement>

		<answer><p>
			In both <xref ref="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e1" text="type-local">part</xref>
			and <xref ref="exercise-orthog-decomp-R2-std-basis-e2" text="type-local">part</xref>,
			the result was exactly the decomposition of <m> \uvec{u} </m> into a linear combination of the standard basis vectors as described in <xref ref="subsection-vectors-concepts-standard-basis" />:
			<md> (x,y) = x \uvec{e}_1 + y \uvec{e}_2 </md>.
		</p></answer>

	</task>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-proj-Rn-axis">
	<title>Orthogonal projection onto an axis in <m> \R^n </m></title>

	<statement><p>
		Compute <m> \proj_{\uvec{e}_j} \uvec{u}  </m> for general <m> n </m>-dimensional vector <m> \uvec{u} = (u_1,u_2,\dotsc,u_n) </m>,
		where <m> \uvec{e}_j </m> is the <m> \nth[j] </m> <term><xref ref="definition-vectors-std-basis" text="custom">standard basis vector</xref></term> in <m> \R^n </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><m> \proj_{\uvec{e}_j} \uvec{u} = u_j \uvec{e}_j </m></p></answer>

</exercise>

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/proj-geometry.ptx" />

<exercise>
	<title>Orthogonality versus vector operations</title>

	<introduction><p>
		Assume all vectors are in <m> \R^n </m>.
		In each case,
		use <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra" text="title" />
		(<xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra" />)
		to verify that the statement is always true.
	</p></introduction>

	<task>
		<title>Orthogonality versus addition</title>
		<p>
		If <m> \uvec{w} </m> is <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term>
		to both <m> \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
		then <m> \uvec{w} </m> is also orthogonal to <m> \uvec{x} </m> for <m> \uvec{x} = \uvec{u} + \uvec{v} </m>.
		</p>
	</task>

	<task>
		<title>Orthogonality versus scalar multiplication</title>
		<p>
		If <m> \uvec{w} </m> is <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term>
		to <m> \uvec{v} </m>,
		then <m> \uvec{w} </m> is also orthogonal to <m> \uvec{x} </m> for <m> \uvec{x} = k \uvec{v} </m> for all scalars <m> k </m>.
		</p>
	</task>

	<task>
		<title>Orthogonality versus linear combination</title>
		<p>
		If <m> \uvec{w} </m> is <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term>
		to each of <m> \uvec{v}_1, \uvec{v}_2, \dotsc, \uvec{v}_m </m>,
		then <m> \uvec{w} </m> is also orthogonal to <m> \uvec{x} </m> for <md> \uvec{x} = k_1 \uvec{v}_1 + k_2 \uvec{v}_2 + \dotsb + k_m \uvec{v}_m </md> for all scalars <m> k_1, k_2, \dotsc, k_m </m>.
		</p>
	</task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Maximum orthogonality</title>

	<introduction><p>
		An <term>orthogonal set</term> of vectors is a collection <m> \{ \uvec{v}_1, \uvec{v}_2, \dotsc, \uvec{v}_m \} </m>
		where each <m> \uvec{v}_i </m> is orthogonal to every <m> \uvec{v}_j </m>, <m> j \neq i </m>.
	</p></introduction>

	<task><p>
		Reasoning geometrically,
		what is the maximum number of vectors possible in an orthogonal set in <m> \R^2 </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Reasoning geometrically,
		what is the maximum number of vectors possible in an orthogonal set in <m> \R^3 </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Based on your answers so far,
		what do you think is the maximum number of vectors possible in an orthogonal set in <m> \R^4 </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Generalizing,
		what do you think is the maximum number of vectors possible in an orthogonal set in <m> \R^n </m>?
	</p></task>

</exercise>

